[Music]  hello everyone and welcome to today's  webinar titled automated 3d light sheet  microscopy opening new dimensions in  neuroscience  this webinar is a part of the 10th  annual neuroscience virtual conference  i am antonina salcido of labrutz and i  will be your moderator for today's event  today's educational web seminar is  presented by lab roots and sponsored by  miltoni biotek  for more information about miltoni  biotek please visit  miltonybiotech.com  now let's get started  before we begin i'd like to remind  everyone that this event is interactive  we encourage you to first participate by  communicating with other attendees using  our new live chat feature during the  presentation  you can find the live chat located at  the right of your screen  you can also participate by submitting  as many questions as you would like  during the presentation  to do so simply type them into the ask a  question box and click send  if you have trouble seeing or hearing  the presentation click on the help desk  button located at the bottom of your  screen located within the navigation bar  or from the lobby  finally as a reminder this presentation  is educational and offers free  continuing education credits  click on the continuing education  credits link located in the abstract  window below the presentation window and  follow the process to obtain your  credits  i now present today's speaker dr amanda  burke field application scientist at  miltoni biotech inc  for a complete biography on our speaker  please visit the biography tab at the  left of your screen  dr burke you may now begin your  presentation  want to thank everyone for joining us  today i'm excited to be given the  opportunity to discuss the power of  light sheet microscopy in the  neuroscience world and introduce you to  milton's newest light sheet microscope  ablaze  here is an outline of what i will cover  in my talk  first i will introduce you to light  cheap microscopy and how this technology  can benefit your imaging workflow  i will then provide you with an  introduction into the ultra microscope  blaze which is our new fully automated  light sheet instrument  i also want to give you a little bit of  context in terms of sample preparation  so i will give an overview of tissue  clearing  why and how we clear  and finally i will touch upon some  practical applications for 3d imaging in  the neuroscience field  so what is light sheet microscopy  light sheet microscope work as virtual  microtomes almost like a tissue scanner  with the ultramicroscope glaze  the sample is illuminated from the side  using a sheet of light the fluorescent  signal is then collected along the  vertical axis something we refer to as  the 90 degree principle  for imaging the sample is moved through  the light sheets as shown in the  animation on the right resulting in a  high resolution 3d data set  but how might this imaging technology be  important or benefit you in your imaging  workflow  if you are currently performing  traditional wide field microscopy you  are likely quite familiar with these  tissue sectioning instruments  as you know opaque tissues can be  into very thin slices something around  10 to 20 microns which ultimately makes  them translucent and it's this  translucent property that makes the  sample suitable for imaging on slides  if you're currently using this strategy  to image your samples you know how  tedious this work can be and not to  mention the damage the tissue can endure  from the suctioning blade as well as  temperature related issues  it is also common that during this  process sections can be lost and  therefore data is lost  it is true that 2d immunofluorescent  imaging is currently the most common  imaging practice  however the data obtained represents the  three-dimensional nature of the biology  in a very limited manner  in comparison renderings of a whole  intact organ can provide the full  picture  this can be observed here in these  images both samples were saying for the  vasculature marker cd31 but it is clear  that the 2d analysis on the left cannot  capture the complexity of the blood  vessels in the mouse brain as one can  appreciate of that on the 3d analysis on  the right  another common method for imaging larger  samples is confocal microscopy which  generates optical sections instead of  physical ones  can focal microscopes do this by a point  scanning mechanism across the sample  since the entire sample in the z  dimension is illuminated a pinhole in  front of the detector is necessary to  eliminate out-of-focus light  so even though you're illuminating the  entire sample volume only fluorescent  signal very close to the focal plane is  resolved and detected  in addition since much of the light from  the sample fluorescence is blocked at  this pinhole the increased resolution  you get from the optical section is at  the cost of decreased signal intensity  so to offset this drop-in signal after  the pinhole the light intensity is  detected by a sensitive detector which  basically serves to transform the light  signal into an electrical one  so while confocal microscopy is a  valuable technique for some samples and  or applications  there are a few drawbacks with regards  to acquisition acquisition speed and the  increased potential for photo bleaching  samples during an imaging session  now to compare with light sheet  microscopy the excitation and detection  paths are uncoupled and oriented  perpendicularly where the excitation  beam illuminates the sample from the  side and the detection occurs on the  vertical axis  in clear tissue the excitation beam can  propagate through the width of the  sample and therefore the light sheet  itself is creating the optical section  this means there's no requirement for  blocking out a focused light with a  pinhole or amplification of the signal  with a sensitive detector  instead light cheap microscopes are able  to acquire the entire field of view in a  single image  this gives way to much faster  acquisition times and it's a lot more  gentle to your sample in terms of photo  bleaching  now that we're all a little more  familiar with light sheet technology i'd  like to introduce miltoni's ultra  microscope light sheet platform we have  three different configurations with the  zoom body here on the bottom left  standing as our original um2 the super  plan which is the second generation um  ii on the bottom right  and then we have the ultra microscope 3  blades on top  all um systems are very easy to use and  flexible to your various imaging needs  the light sheets are optimized in these  systems to provide you with homogeneous  illumination of your sample this is  largely due to the fact that there are a  total of six sheets three on the left  and three on the right  they're all compatible with all clearing  protocols i will speak to this a little  more in the coming slides  and they can accommodate both small  samples like organoids of only a couple  millimeters or larger samples like an  entire mouse brain  and with the newest member of the  ultramicroscope family the blaze  you can image samples as large as an  entire adult mouse  i will now continue to focus on the  blaze and i really want to highlight  some innovative features that have been  incorporated into this specific um model  the blaze is fully automated light cheap  microscope which eliminates several pain  points that can occur during your  imaging sessions  with some of these features  first the automated sample release  giving you easy and fast access to your  samples with effortless sample mounting  the automated objective lens changer the  objectives live on the instrument so  there is no need for mounting and  dismounting this dramatically reduces  the potential for damaging these  objectives  and then we have the automated focusing  feature  there are no course or fine adjustment  knobs focusing in on your sample is  accomplished with just a click of the  button in the software  much of this automation is conducted  through the automated detection unit  which is controlled completely through  the software  inside the unit itself there are four  intermediate lenses providing a range of  magnifications that can be applied to  each objective  we have the multi-immersion plan  objectives this system is equipped with  a 1.1 x a 4x and a 12x objective  these lenses are specifically designed  for light sheet microscopy having the  longest working distances relative to  their numerical aperture  we have our cutting edge light sheet  optics as mentioned previously there are  a total of six light sheets two central  beams with four additional sheets  directed at an inward angle this is to  reduce shadowing effects and ultimately  resulting in homogeneous illumination of  your sample  and then we have that large automated  chamber which provides plenty of space  to either mount multiple smaller samples  for batch imaging as shown in the image  here or a very large sample something  like an entire human kidney  one feature i mentioned earlier is that  the ultra microscopes are compatible  with all clearing protocols but why do  we clear tissues and how is tissue  clearing accomplished  so let's start with the question why  in a given sample light passage is  highly impaired due to things like  reflection refraction scattering and  absorption as it encounters different  materials within the tissue  these materials are largely referring to  water proteins and lipids  and each of these molecules have a  different refractive index  the term refractive index which is a  term you will hear quite often with  regards to tissue clearing  is a numerical value that is assigned to  these different molecules and represents  the speed at which light travels through  a given material for example water has a  refractive index of 1.3  proteins around 1.5 and lipids around  1.4  this number is an expression of how much  slower light moves through a substance  compared to that of a vacuum  the higher the number the slower the  light travels in that material  so the goal of tissue clearing is to  render the tissue transparent but what  does transparency really mean  it is essentially the result of  homogenizing or matching the refractive  index of the entire sample  so the reason we clear tissue is to  remove light scattering molecules and  essentially alter the tissue composition  so that the light's behavior is  normalized as it travels through the  sample  so if you look into liver now you will  notice there are many different clearing  protocols all a little unique in their  mechanism for homogenizing the  refractive index  but here i will introduce you to three  major classes  that being the hydrophilic clearing  methods hydrogel face clearing methods  and then hydrophobic or also known as  organic clearing methods  hydrophilic clearing techniques start by  removing lipids with some form of  detergent the samples are then placed  into high concentration urea solution  and imaged in an ri matched aqueous  immersion  the resulting refractive index of  samples cleared  in this method range from about 1.38 to  1.48  so these methods are typically  quite simple and can clear tissues  rather well while also preserving  endogenous fluorescence  the main disadvantage  is the time it takes to clear samples  which with this protocol can take weeks  and it isn't always as effective as  other methods like the organic solvents  or the hydrogel-based clearing  on dense samples  hydrogel clearing methods start by  crosslinking proteins to a hydrogel  matrix for initial sample stabilization  lipids are then extracted with some  detergents  and it's this portion of the protocol  that can take quite some time when done  passively  as an alternate  and to speed up this process  active clearing can be conducted which  utilizes an electrophoresis operation  the resulting refractive index of  samples cleared with this method is  around 1.45  so people select this method um largely  because  it is non-toxic it is compatible with  immunolabeling and it also maintains  that endogenous fluorescence  some of the big disadvantages here are  typically costs associated with both the  imaging solutions  and commercial electrophoresis systems  to  ultimately achieve that active clearing  hydrophobic or also often referred to as  organic clearing methods first dehydrate  the samples using some alcohol gradient  where lipids are also extracted and  they're finally cleared in a high  refractive index organic solvent around  1.55  which is also similar to that of the  refractive index of proteins  the major advantages to organic clearing  methods is that the resulting  transparency is quite robust compared to  other methods and is relatively easy and  cost-effective workflow to adopt  they're widely used in neuroscience  really rendering those brains  quite transparent  the disadvantage here is that most of  these hydrophobic methods use toxic  chemicals and that final clearing step  which some users  like to avoid  so when it comes to tissue clearing i  hope i convey there is not a  one-size-fits-all protocol  selection of your clearing method will  depend on the tissue species your  intended application amongst other  considerations  there will always be the possibility  that you may have to adapt or modify  your protocol to achieve true refractive  index homogenized samples  not all protocols will render samples  clear enough for light sheet and  therefore may not be compatible with  this technology  but there are a lot of resources out  there including myself to help you  navigate and find the method most  suitable for your application  now that you have a general idea about  tissue clearing i want to shift gears a  little bit to antibody selection when it  comes to 3d  immunofluorescence i think it's  important to point out that the time and  effort to optimize traditional  immunolabeling conditions for 2d imaging  on slides can differ quite a bit when  applied to large samples in combination  with clearing  we will use this cerebral organoid  stained with beta tubulin iii as an  example  the left image was the result of  immunolabeling under the specifications  intended for 2d immunofluorescence this  pattern of antibody bound largely to the  perimeter of the sample is a common  result for many of those new to 3d  imaging  from this point you would have to  optimize the immunolabeling conditions  by trying to understand if this effect  is due to insufficient permeabilization  methodology  the wrong antibody concentration  or antibody incubation times  so though the image on the right was  stained with the same beta tubulin 3  antibody the conditions have already  been optimized for 3d in-text samples  where you now observe proper diffusion  of the antibody into the interior of the  organoid  so if there's an option to purchase an  antibody that has been previously  validated for 3d immunofluorescent  applications either by another lab or a  vendor taking this route can save you  both time and effort  another consideration that can also  complicate antibody optimization efforts  is if you're using an indirect method  for immunolabeling with the primary  antibody against your target which is  recognized by a secondary antibody  conjugated to that fluorescent dye  you can see the timelines outlined here  where indirect labeling systems can  increase your protocol by a week or  maybe even more  so if you have the option to go with the  primary antibody that has the  fluorescent dye directly conjugated to  it it's in your best interest not only  does this decrease the protocol timeline  and simplifies optimization efforts  because it's one antibody instead of two  but we have also seen with our direct  conjugate primary antibodies that signal  to background ratios are enhanced as you  can see in this comparison image on the  right  to address this bottleneck in tissue  clearing in 3d immunofluorescent  workflows miltoni biotech has created a  simple and streamlined solution to help  you get up and started with light sheet  imaging  first we have our max tissue clearing  kit on the left these reagents have been  developed for the hydrophobic clearing  of tissues and of an important note this  kit uses non-toxic organic solvents for  clearing and imaging  we are also providing an ever growing  list of antibodies that have been  validated for 3d immunofluorescence  some important highlights to milton's 3d  antibody portfolio is that they are all  directly conjugated to a small  photostable die  and they are recompenently engineered so  you can feel confident that you'll get  that lot to lock consistency between  experiments  our research and development department  has worked really hard to develop a full  solution to your 3d imaging needs this  includes predefined protocols that are  supplied on our web page for free for  the optimal immunostaining and clearing  of different tissue species  here is just an example of a workflow  for one of our protocols that will walk  you step by step through the  immunolabeling portion and the clearing  portion of intact mouse brains  we are now moving into the last portion  of my talk where i will review some  neuroscience-based applications using  the ultra microscope but of course  there's potential for much more than  what i will show you here  i hope that you feel free to reach out  to me i'll provide my contact  information at the end of this  presentation or you can join us at the  booth afterwards if you want to discuss  ways of enhancing your research projects  by incorporating light sheet microscopy  so a first hit on some of the most  popular areas of neuroscience research  where light sheet has proven to really  be a powerful tool  in alzheimer's and parkinson's disease  research  the 3d imaging of intact mouse brains is  allowing researchers to quantify plaques  and 